PART C -- IS IT SELF-CONSISTENT?  The two anchors, and the family of profiles that could serve both.
==========================================================================================================================
  ANCHOR 1 (cluster): M_X = 4.002e+14 Msun inside 1000 kpc, where M_bar = 6.402e+13  =>  ratio 5.73
  ANCHOR 2 (pair)   : M_X = 4.781e+12 Msun inside 132 kpc, where M_bar = 1.549e+11  =>  ratio 30.9

  A1 -- can ONE law R(r) = M_X/M_bar in absolute radius serve both?  The clusters MEASURE R(r) at eleven
       radii, so the pair's own radius is inside the measured cluster range and no extrapolation is needed:
         r [kpc]      30      40      50      75     100     150     200     300     420     750    1000
       R_cluster    3.89    6.96    7.62    8.37    8.78    9.39    9.45    8.77    8.07    6.47    5.73
       +/- (sem)    4.42    2.29    2.23    1.96    1.66    1.17    0.82    0.57    0.45    0.33    0.20
       at the pair's own 132 kpc the clusters measure R = 9.20 +/- 1.30; the pairs require 30.9 +/- 1.5
       the cluster profile is itself NON-MONOTONE: it rises from 3.9 at 30 kpc to 9.5 at 200 kpc and falls to 5.73 at 1000 kpc
  [FAIL] A1 a single radial law R(r) in ABSOLUTE radius serves both the cluster and the pair anchor   (at the same 132 kpc the clusters give 9.20 +/- 1.30 and the pairs need 30.9 +/- 1.5, a factor 3.4 at 11 sigma -- so the factor 5.7 of L21's S1 is NOT an artefact of comparing different radii; it survives at 3.4 when the radius is held fixed)
  
  A2 -- in SCALED radius.  The cluster anchor sits at r/R200 = 0.53 (R200 = 1879 kpc).
       The pair's R200 is not measured, so it is SCANNED over the whole plausible range rather than assumed,
       and the comparison is reported for every value:
        pair R200 [kpc]   r_p/R200  matched cluster radius   cluster R there   factor
                    250      0.529                  994 kpc              5.74     5.37
                    300      0.441                  829 kpc              6.20     4.98
                    350      0.378                  710 kpc              6.60     4.67
                    400      0.331                  622 kpc              6.95     4.44
                    450      0.294                  552 kpc              7.27     4.25
                    500      0.265                  497 kpc              7.57     4.08
                    600      0.220                  414 kpc              8.10     3.81
  [FAIL] A2 a single radial law in SCALED radius r/R200 serves both anchors, for SOME admissible pair R200   (the gap is 3.8-5.4x over the whole range of pair R200 from 250 to 600 kpc; scaling the radius does not help and mostly makes it WORSE than A1's factor, because the cluster ratio profile is falling over the matched range)
  
  [FAIL] A3 a component that TRACES the baryons at ONE universal ratio (the cosmic-share reading taken literally) serves both anchors   (30.9 +/- 1.5 against 5.73 +/- 0.68, 15 sigma.  This is the single most important consequence of L7 + L21 together: the cosmic share is a CLUSTER-scale coincidence, not a universal component)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE SEARCH.  The most general host-blind rule mapping a system's baryons to the source is a function of
  the enclosed baryon mass and the radius; its scale-free form is

        M_X( < r )  =  C * M_bar( < r )^a * (r / 100 kpc)^b ,

  which CONTAINS every proposal this programme has made: (a, b) = (1, 0) is 'X traces the baryons', a = 0
  is a universal background halo, a = 1 with b < 0 is a kernel-like boost decaying outward.  The two
  anchors fix one LINE in (a, b); the family is scanned along it and every other gate applied to each
  member.  A member passing all of them would be a live candidate.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  the anchor line: 6.0240 a + 2.0227 b = 4.4272,  i.e.  b = 2.1887 - 2.9782 a
  continuous baryon profiles built for the five weak-lensing clusters (A1795, A2029, A2142, A85, ZW1215) on a 700-point log grid, 2 kpc - 30 Mpc
  P0 positive control: a dark component with an NFW shape, projected through THIS machinery, gives a shear log-slope -0.820 against the measured -0.851 +/- 0.040
  [PASS] P0 [positive control] the shape gate is PASSABLE: a dark component with an NFW shape reproduces the measured shear log-slope, so a FAIL below is a statement about the candidate and not about the gate   (NFW gives -0.820, measured -0.851 +/- 0.040 (0.5 sigma))
  P1 reproduction: the FRAMEWORK's own phantom, projected through the same machinery, gives a shear log-slope -0.309 (per cluster -0.37, -0.37, -0.27, -0.28, -0.26) against the measured -0.851 +/- 0.040
  [FAIL] P1 [reproduction of L24 C11] the framework's own projected shear log-slope agrees with the measured one, i.e. its phantom has the right lensing SHAPE   (-0.309 against -0.851 +/- 0.040, a difference of +0.542 at 9 sigma on L24's quoted error -- independently reproducing L24 C11's +0.53 +/- 0.06 at 9 sigma, with different code and a different baryon build.  L24's new shape constraint therefore enters the specification VERIFIED)

