  [PASS] B1 the two-anchor family has a member reproducing the cluster's OWN measured ratio profile to 0.15 dex   (best rms = 0.132 dex at a = +0.50; admissible window a in [+0.40, +0.50])
  [PASS] B2 it has a member whose projected shear log-slope agrees with the measured one within 3 sigma   (the family's positive-shear members span -2.00 to +0.26 against a measured -0.851 +/- 0.040; admissible window a in [+0.60, +0.70], closest member a = +0.60 at -0.802; every member with a < -0.6 produces shear of the WRONG SIGN)
  [PASS] B3 it has a member passing the galaxy-scale non-overshoot gate at L* AND at the dwarf scale   (admissible window a in [-1.60, -0.80], and every member in it is one of the wrong-sign-shear members: the only way to keep the source out of a 2e9 Msun galaxy while feeding the pairs is to make it rise outward, which destroys the lensing signal)
  [FAIL] B4 [THE SEARCH] some single member of the family passes B1, B2 and B3 at once, i.e. a single radial profile serves both the cluster and the pair scale and survives the shear and galaxy gates   (ratio-profile window a in [+0.40, +0.50], shear window a in [+0.60, +0.70], galaxy window a in [-1.60, -0.80]; the intersection is EMPTY.  The windows that DO open are disjoint: the member that reproduces the cluster's ratio profile is not the member that projects to the right shear slope, and neither is admissible inside a dwarf)

  B5 -- the one escape the anchors leave: the pairs' baryons are UNDERCOUNTED.  For a component at the
       cosmic share to give the observed pair kinematics, the true baryon mass inside 132 kpc must be 4.96x
       the K-band stellar mass, i.e. 7.68e+11 Msun -- 6.13e+11 of it undetected.  Does the framework's
       OWN galaxy-scale gate close that door?  Priced with the distribution that is MOST favourable to the
       escape (uniform density inside the pair separation, which minimises what lands in the inner galaxy):
         uniform: 2.65e+08 Msun inside 10 kpc = 0.002 M_b per galaxy (gate at 10 kpc: 0.41)
         uniform: 2.12e+09 Msun inside 20 kpc = 0.014 M_b per galaxy (gate at 10 kpc: 0.41)
         uniform: 7.15e+09 Msun inside 30 kpc = 0.046 M_b per galaxy (gate at 10 kpc: 0.41)
  [FAIL] B5 the framework's own galaxy-scale gate CLOSES the undetected-baryon escape at pair scale   (it does not: even 5.0x the K-band baryons, spread uniformly inside 132 kpc, puts only 0.0017 M_b inside 10 kpc against a tolerance of 0.41.  This door is OPEN on this repository's data and is closed, if at all, by circumgalactic-medium mass budgets (the requirement is ~4 stellar masses of gas inside 130 kpc of an L* pair), which is a literature question this lane does not settle and does not claim to)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE INCOMPATIBLE PAIR.  Along the anchor line the surviving direction is a HOST-MASS law: the source's
  abundance per baryon falls with host mass.  That exponent is not free -- the pair sample MEASURES it
  internally, in quartiles of its own baryonic mass, with the same estimator.  NOT at fixed radius --
  see the caveat below, and C2, which controls for separation and is the load-bearing version.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      log M_b = 10.87  N =  475  median r_p =   106 kpc  M_dark/M_bar within r_p =  26.3 +/- 2.9
      log M_b = 11.11  N =  475  median r_p =   116 kpc  M_dark/M_bar within r_p =  26.5 +/- 2.9
      log M_b = 11.27  N =  475  median r_p =   148 kpc  M_dark/M_bar within r_p =  31.3 +/- 3.7
      log M_b = 11.42  N =  475  median r_p =   165 kpc  M_dark/M_bar within r_p =  38.4 +/- 4.0
  
      d log(M_X/M_bar) / d log M_bar measured INSIDE the pair sample : +0.298 +/- 0.113
      the same exponent demanded by the cluster-to-pair anchors      : -0.280 +/- 0.008
      the two have OPPOSITE SIGN and differ at 5.1 sigma
  [FAIL] C1 [the incompatible pair, one dimension] the host-mass exponent the two anchors demand agrees with the host-mass exponent the pair sample measures internally, so a host-mass abundance law can serve both scales   (internal +0.298 +/- 0.113 against the anchors' -0.280 +/- 0.008: 5.1 sigma, and of OPPOSITE SIGN.  Within the pair population the source's abundance per baryon RISES with host mass; between pairs and clusters it must FALL by a factor 5.4.  CAVEAT, stated because it weakens this check: r_p correlates with M_b across these quartiles, so part of the +0.30 is radial.  C2 below controls for that and the mass exponent alone falls to +0.15 +/- 0.12; the robust statement is C2's joint one, not this one, and this check should be read as indicative)

  C2 -- the same test in BOTH exponents at once.  The pair sample can measure the WHOLE rule internally:
       split it three ways in baryonic mass and three ways in separation and fit
          log R = const + alpha log M_bar + beta log r      (so a = 1 + alpha, b = beta),
       then ask whether that (a, b) lies on the anchor line the cluster and pair scales together demand.
                log M_b          log r_p      N     M_dark/M_bar
                  10.91             1.60    267        16.4 +/- 2.1
                  10.94             2.12    210        31.8 +/- 4.5
                  10.93             2.52    156        59.7 +/-17.3
                  11.18             1.64    203        14.1 +/- 1.9
                  11.20             2.11    211        38.1 +/- 6.2
