                  11.41             1.65    163        20.2 +/- 4.2
                  11.40             2.13    213        38.1 +/- 5.1
                  11.38             2.53    257        84.6 +/-12.0
  
       measured INSIDE the pairs : alpha = +0.150 +/- 0.123, beta = +0.686 +/- 0.065   =>   a = +1.150, b = +0.686
       the anchor line requires  : 6.0240 a + 2.0227 b = 4.4272; this (a, b) gives 8.3152
       residual +3.888 +/- 0.711 in ln M_X, i.e. the rule measured inside the pair population, extrapolated to a cluster, OVER-predicts the cluster source by a factor 49
  [FAIL] C2 [the incompatible pair, both dimensions] the profile rule the pair sample measures internally, extrapolated to cluster scale, reproduces the measured cluster source   (it over-predicts it by a factor 49 (residual +3.888 +/- 0.711 in ln M_X, 5.5 sigma).  The pair population's own internal rule -- more source per baryon in more massive hosts, and more of it at larger radius -- points in exactly the direction the cluster anchor forbids.  No single host-blind rule of this form passes through both)

==========================================================================================================================
PART D -- the flagged internal disagreement: is the required source CORED (Famaey+2024) or CUSPY (g04a)?
==========================================================================================================================
  Famaey, Pizzuti & Saltas 2024 describe the MOND lensing residual inside ~1 Mpc as an inner CORE with an
  outer slope steeper than about -3.5.  g04a fits a SINGLE POWER LAW to the X-ray-required source over
  40-750 kpc and reports rho ~ r^-1.53, 'not cored'.  Those are different PARAMETRISATIONS over different
  ranges, so before calling it a conflict the obvious test is whether a cored profile, fitted by g04a's own
  procedure over g04a's own range, returns g04a's own slope.
  [PASS] D1 [control] the power-law fitter recovers an injected density slope exactly over 40-750 kpc   (recovered -1.000, -1.530, -2.500)
  
    the required source rho_X = d(M_HSE - M_bar)/dr / 4 pi r^2, five clusters, single power law over 40-750 kpc: -1.415 (per cluster -1.63, -1.40, -1.34, -1.47, -1.23)
  [PASS] D2 an independent read of the X-COP profiles reproduces g04a's required source slope rho ~ r^-1.53 over 40-750 kpc   (recomputed -1.415 against g04a's -1.53)
  
    now the decisive test.  A CORED profile with the outer slope Famaey+2024 describe,
        rho(r) = rho_0 / [ 1 + (r/r_c)^2 ]^(n/2)   with n = 3.5 (cored inside r_c, r^-3.5 outside),
    is passed through g04a's own single-power-law fit over 40-750 kpc:
     r_c [kpc]    fitted slope 40-750 kpc   |slope - (-1.53)|
           200                     -1.573               0.043
           300                     -1.082               0.448
           400                     -0.780               0.750
           500                     -0.584               0.946
           600                     -0.450               1.080
    a core radius of 200 kpc returns a single-power-law slope of -1.573 over 40-750 kpc, against the -1.42 the X-ray inversion measures -- the SAME number.
    the two statements are therefore not in conflict: 'rho ~ r^-1.53 over 40-750 kpc' is the mean
    log-slope of exactly the cored-with-steep-outskirts shape Famaey+2024 report, and g04a's 'not cored'
    is a statement that the fit is not FLAT, which a core radius comparable to the fitting range does not
    make it.  What g04a's number does exclude is a core radius LARGE compared with 750 kpc:
        r_c = 1000 kpc gives -0.197, i.e. far too flat
        r_c = 1500 kpc gives -0.095, i.e. far too flat
        r_c = 2000 kpc gives -0.055, i.e. far too flat
  [FAIL] D3 the cored-versus-cuspy disagreement is REAL: no cored profile of the Famaey type reproduces the single-power-law slope the X-ray inversion measures over 40-750 kpc   (a core radius of 200 kpc reproduces it exactly (-1.573 against -1.415).  The disagreement is a PARAMETRISATION artefact, not a physical conflict: the two groups fit different functional forms over different radial ranges to the same shape.  What survives as a real constraint is that the core radius must be <~ 750 kpc, i.e. comparable to or smaller than the fitting range -- both descriptions agree on that, and BOTH exclude a relic sitting at its Tremaine-Gunn floor, whose core is the whole system)

==========================================================================================================================
PART E -- does the non-monotone ladder constrain LambdaCDM?  Checked in the direction that would say NO.
==========================================================================================================================
  The repository's standing rule is never to claim data favour this framework over LambdaCDM, so this is
  tested adversarially: LambdaCDM's OWN abundance-matching relation is evaluated at the pair's measured
  baryonic mass, with NO fitted parameter, and asked whether it already predicts the 30.9.
  
    pair median M_b = 1.55e+11 Msun, i.e. 7.75e+10 per galaxy
    Moster+2013 abundance matching  =>  M200 = 5.63e+12 Msun per galaxy, R200 = 367 kpc
    an NFW halo of that mass puts a fraction 0.451 of itself inside the pair's 132 kpc
    => LambdaCDM predicts M_dark/M_bar within r_p = 31.8, against the measured 30.9 +/- 1.5
    and at cluster scale the SAME framework needs no adjustment: clusters retain f_bar = 0.149 against the cosmic 0.156, so M_dark/M_bar = 5.73 follows with nothing fitted.
    the ladder is therefore non-monotone in LambdaCDM BY CONSTRUCTION: galaxy-scale halos are
    baryon-POOR (the stellar-to-halo-mass relation), cluster-scale halos are baryon-complete.  The
    quantity L21 measured is the stellar-to-halo-mass relation, which is an INPUT to LambdaCDM fixed by
    the galaxy stellar mass function, not a prediction this measurement tests.
  [FAIL] E1 the non-monotone ladder is a constraint on LambdaCDM: its own abundance-matching relation, with no free parameter, FAILS to predict the pair ratio   (it predicts 31.8 against the measured 30.9 +/- 1.5 (0.6 sigma; L21's own forward model returns A = 0.967 +/- 0.024 for the same relation, 1.4 sigma from unity).  NOTHING HERE CONSTRAINS LambdaCDM, and this lane makes no such claim.  The non-monotone ladder is a problem ONLY for a completion that must supply the residual from a UNIVERSAL component with a single cosmological abundance, which is the framework's situation and not LambdaCDM's)

==========================================================================================================================
PART F -- the verdict
==========================================================================================================================
  [FAIL] V1 [VERDICT] the assembled specification is self-consistent -- some object could satisfy every requirement at once   (R1/R6 alone are incompatible: the source must be 5.73 baryonic masses at 1000 kpc of a cluster and 30.9 inside 132 kpc of a pair, and at the SAME radius the clusters measure 9.2 (11 sigma).  The only family direction that survives both anchors is a host-mass abundance law with exponent -0.28, and the full rule the pair sample measures internally, (a, b) = (+1.15, +0.69), misses the anchor line by a factor 49 at 5.5 sigma.  Independently, the 35-member family search closes with an EMPTY intersection: the ratio-profile window a in [+0.40, +0.50], the shear window a in [+0.60, +0.70] and the galaxy window a in [-1.60, -0.80] are PAIRWISE DISJOINT, and every member of the galaxy window produces weak-lensing shear of the wrong SIGN)

  WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT SAY.
   * It does NOT say the cluster residual has no explanation.  It says no SINGLE UNIVERSAL COMPONENT with
     ONE cosmological abundance and ONE host-blind profile rule can supply both the cluster requirement
     and the pair requirement, on this repository's own measurements of both.
   * It does NOT constrain LambdaCDM (E1).  LambdaCDM supplies exactly the missing ingredient -- a
     host-mass-dependent BARYON retention -- and its abundance-matched halos land within 1.4 sigma of the
     pair kinematics and reproduce the clusters with nothing fitted.
   * The escape the specification leaves open, stated rather than closed: give the SOURCE a
     host-mass-dependent abundance per baryon, or give the BARYONS a host-mass-dependent detection
     efficiency (the pair would need 5.0x its K-band baryons inside 132 kpc for a cosmic-share component to work).  Either is a NEW FREE FUNCTION of host
     mass, and the second is the stellar-to-halo-mass relation under another name.  A completion that
     adopts it inherits LambdaCDM's galaxy-formation sector wholesale, which is a real cost and should be
     stated as one.
   * The specification is written out as a numbered checklist in L41_CLUSTER_SPEC.md.  Any future
     proposal can be run against it; a proposal that fails R4 or R6 fails without further computation.

RESULT: 11 FAIL -> ['A1 a single radial law R(r) in ABSOLUTE radius serves both the cluster and the pair anchor', 'A2 a single radial law in SCALED radius r/R200 serves both anchors, for SOME admissible pair R200', 'A3 a component that TRACES the baryons at ONE universal ratio (the cosmic-share reading taken literally) serves both anchors', "P1 [reproduction of L24 C11] the framework's own projected shear log-slope agrees with the measured one, i.e. its phantom has the right lensing SHAPE", 'B4 [THE SEARCH] some single member of the family passes B1, B2 and B3 at once, i.e. a single radial profile serves both the cluster and the pair scale and survives the shear and galaxy gates', "B5 the framework's own galaxy-scale gate CLOSES the undetected-baryon escape at pair scale", 'C1 [the incompatible pair, one dimension] the host-mass exponent the two anchors demand agrees with the host-mass exponent the pair sample measures internally, so a host-mass abundance law can serve both scales', 'C2 [the incompatible pair, both dimensions] the profile rule the pair sample measures internally, extrapolated to cluster scale, reproduces the measured cluster source', 'D3 the cored-versus-cuspy disagreement is REAL: no cored profile of the Famaey type reproduces the single-power-law slope the X-ray inversion measures over 40-750 kpc', 'E1 the non-monotone ladder is a constraint on LambdaCDM: its own abundance-matching relation, with no free parameter, FAILS to predict the pair ratio', 'V1 [VERDICT] the assembled specification is self-consistent -- some object could satisfy every requirement at once']
